WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.062
Every externally controlled value needs
runtime validation before application code

2
00:00:05.142 --> 00:00:05.830
relies on it.

3
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.284
That includes path parameters, query
strings, request bodies,

4
00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:11.114
and security-relevant headers.

5
00:00:11.194 --> 00:00:14.319
TypeScript annotations describe what
developers intend,

6
00:00:14.399 --> 00:00:17.274
but HTTP delivers bytes and parsed
JavaScript values.

7
00:00:17.354 --> 00:00:21.604
A type assertion such as treating request
body as UpdateUserRequest changes the

8
00:00:21.684 --> 00:00:25.683
compiler's view; it performs no runtime
check and emits no validating code.

9
00:00:25.763 --> 00:00:28.576
The server must examine the actual value
it received.

10
00:00:28.656 --> 00:00:32.405
Check required and optional fields,
primitive types, string lengths,

11
00:00:32.485 --> 00:00:35.923
numeric ranges, enumerated values, arrays,
and nested objects.

12
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:40.440
Reject unexpected properties when they
would create ambiguity or expose behavior.

13
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.832
Avoid implicit coercion that turns
surprising strings into numbers or booleans.

14
00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:46.537
When normalization is needed,

15
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.554
such as trimming text or canonicalizing an
identifier,

16
00:00:49.634 --> 00:00:54.134
perform it deliberately and validate the
canonical form so later layers reason about

17
00:00:54.214 --> 00:00:55.339
one representation.

18
00:00:55.419 --> 00:00:59.481
Schema libraries and framework-native
validators can express these rules,

19
00:00:59.561 --> 00:01:01.686
but the design is library-independent.

20
00:01:01.766 --> 00:01:04.891
Parse unknown input into a validated data
transfer object,

21
00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:07.283
handle failure as an ordinary client
error,

22
00:01:07.363 --> 00:01:10.175
and give the rest of the application a
narrower value.

23
00:01:10.255 --> 00:01:14.630
Validate early enough that malformed data
cannot reach business logic or database

24
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.460
queries that assume a safe shape.

25
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.290
Client-side validation may mirror the
constraints for fast feedback,

26
00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:24.057
but the server repeats the decision
because the client is bypassable.

27
00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:25.824
Keep three questions distinct.

28
00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:28.717
Validation asks whether data has an
acceptable form,

29
00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:31.546
such as whether quantity is an integer
within range.

30
00:01:31.626 --> 00:01:36.126
Authorization asks whether this
authenticated subject may perform the operation on

31
00:01:36.206 --> 00:01:37.019
this resource.

32
00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:41.473
Business-rule enforcement asks whether the
requested transition is allowed now,

33
00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:43.803
such as whether an order is still
editable.

34
00:01:43.883 --> 00:01:46.383
A request can pass one control and fail
another.

35
00:01:46.463 --> 00:01:50.713
Clear separation produces focused tests
and fewer security gaps hidden behind a

36
00:01:50.793 --> 00:01:52.980
vague claim that the input was validated.
